AIM:

To develop a simple Employee Management module using Spring Core in a single Java file, demonstrating IoC and DI using annotations.

PROCEDURE:
1. Create a Maven project in Eclipse
2. Add Spring dependency
3. Create a single Java class
4. Use inner classes for Employee and Service
5. Apply @Component, @Autowired
6. Run the program


CODE:
package com.example;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.BeanFactory;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.*;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class MainApp {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        BeanFactory factory = new AnnotationConfigApplicationContext(AppConfig.class);

        EmployeeService service = factory.getBean(EmployeeService.class);

        service.addEmployee(1, "Arun", "IT");
        service.addEmployee(2, "Divya", "HR");
        service.addEmployee(3, "Karthik", "Finance");

        service.displayEmployees();
    }

    // 🔹 Configuration Class
    @Configuration
    @ComponentScan(basePackages = "com.example")
    static class AppConfig {
    }

    // 🔹 Employee Class
    @Component
    static class Employee {
        private int id;
        private String name;
        private String department;

        public Employee() {}

        public Employee(int id, String name, String department) {
            this.id = id;
            this.name = name;
            this.department = department;
        }

        public int getId() { return id; }
        public void setId(int id) { this.id = id; }

        public String getName() { return name; }
        public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

        public String getDepartment() { return department; }
        public void setDepartment(String department) { this.department = department; }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return id + " - " + name + " - " + department;
        }
    }

    // 🔹 Service Class
    @Component
    static class EmployeeService {

        private List<Employee> empList = new ArrayList<>();

        @Autowired
        private Employee employee;

        public void addEmployee(int id, String name, String dept) {
            Employee emp = new Employee(id, name, dept);
            empList.add(emp);
        }

        public void displayEmployees() {
            System.out.println("Employee List:");
            for (Employee e : empList) {
                System.out.println(e);
            }
        }
    }
}


RESULT:

Thus, a simple Employee Management module was successfully developed using Spring Core in a single Java file.
The concepts of Inversion of Control (IoC) and Dependency Injection (DI) were implemented using @Component and @Autowired annotations.
The Spring container managed the beans using BeanFactory, and employee data was stored and retrieved from memory using a list.
The application executed successfully and displayed the employee details as expected.
